  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Mahavir Prasad Agarwal S/O Radha Kishan ... vs State Of Rajasthan on 25 July, 2019 Author: Pankaj Bhandari Bench: Pankaj Bhandari High Court Of Judicature For Rajasthan Bench At Jaipur S.B. Criminal Miscellaneous (Petition) No. 3160/2019 1. Mahavir Prasad Agarwal S/o Radha Kishan Gupta, Partner Of M/s Shiv Agencies B-32, Atish Market, Jaipur, Raj. 2. M/s Shiv Agencies, Through Its Partner Shri Mahavir Prasad Agarwal, B-32, Atish Market, Jaipur, Raj. 3. Tarachand Jain S/o Jayanti Prasad Jain, Partner Of M/s Tarachand Nareshchand B.r. Street, Alwar. 4. Nareshchand Jain S/o Jayanti Prasad Jain, Partner Of M/s Tarachand Nareshchand B.r. Street, Alwar. 5. Tarachand Nareshchand, Through Its Partner Shri Tarachand Jain , B.r. Street, Alwar. ----Petitioners Versus State Of Rajasthan, Through Pp. ----Respondent For Petitioner(s) : Mr. Ashish Sharma. For State : Mr. Ganesh Saini, Pp. Hon'Ble Mr. Justice Pankaj Bhandari Order 25/07/2019 1.",
    "facts": "In brief facts of the case are that a complaint was filed by Food Inspector against the petitioners for alleging offences under Sections 7(1) and 7(3) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, read with Rule 50(1)/16 of the P.F.A. Rules. 3. Pouches of Gutkha were seized by the Food Inspector. The said samples were sent to the State and the State Analyst has (2 of 3) [CRLMP-3160/2019] submitted a report that samples were adulterated as it did not conform to the prevention of P.F.A. Rules 1958. Thereafter, a complaint was filed. Petitioners applied for sending the samples to Central Food Laboratory. Application was allowed by the Court vide order dated 23.08.2012. 4. It is contended by counsel for the petitioners that valuable rights have been given to the accused under Sub-Section (2) of Section 13 to get the report from the Central Food Laboratory. In exercise of the said right, petitioners have applied the application and Court has allowed the application and has directed that the samples be sent to Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Orders were passed by the Court under Sub-Section (2) of Section 13 of the P.F.A. Act in 2012. From order dated 17.07.2018, it is revealed that the samples have not been produced before the Court till date and have not been sent to the Central Food Laboratory till date. 8. In view of the fact that shelf life of the product was only two months, the samples were drawn on 27.05.2010 by the Food Inspector, a period of nine years has lapsed. Continuation of proceedings would tantamount of abuse of process of Court and would result in grave injustice to the petitioners as their valuable right for getting the samples analyzed by Central Food Laboratory has been infringed.",
    "arguments": "It is contended that the samples were found to be adulterated and in matters involving the adulteration, Court should not exercise the inherent powers.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "It is contended that the samples were found to be adulterated and in matters involving the adulteration, Court should not exercise the inherent powers.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
